From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Junod <donujp@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 13:41:10 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] fix(init): fail torrent initialization when a file cannot be
 sized

During initialization rqbit calls TorrentStorage::ensure_file_length for
every selected file. If that fails (ENOSPC, EDQUOT, a read-only
filesystem, ...) the error is only written to the log with warn!() and
initialization carries on, so the torrent still ends up paused/live as if
nothing happened. The only trace of the failure is a log line; stats and
the torrent state never show it.

Move the loop into a small fallible helper that returns the first
failure, with the file name and requested length as context, and let the
existing error path in ManagedTorrent::start put the torrent into the
Error state. Which files are sized (selection, padding files skipped) is
unchanged, and files after the failing one are not touched.

This interacts with "Allow pausing during the initial file check": that
code treats any initialization error as a pause when pause was requested
(`if init.is_pause_requested()`), so a real sizing failure that races
with a pause would be swallowed again. Give the checksum loop's
cancellation a typed InitialCheckPaused error and suppress only that
when pause was requested; any other error still moves the torrent to
the Error state.

Add tests for the helper (injected StorageFull on the first file) and
for the pause classification.
